MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russia's foreign ministry said on Friday it had fired five staff members of the Polish embassy in Moscow in response to the expulsion of three Russian diplomats from Warsaw. This is according to the release by Anadolu agency on Friday, April 23, 2021.
In a statement posted on the Russian Foreign Ministry's website, it said it had declared five employees of the Polish Embassy in Russia "dishonest". The move is in retaliation for the expulsion of three Russian diplomats from Poland on April 23 in solidarity with the United States.
Last week, Moscow and Washington announced new sanctions against each other, with both countries expelling 10 diplomats and blacklisting more officials.
Washington has tightened economic sanctions against Russia, while Moscow has tightened labor laws for the US embassy and banned the activities of state-sponsored US NGOs in Russia.
The ministry said that in response to Poland's provocative actions in the expulsion of Russian diplomats on the basis of equality, the ministry declared five employees of the Embassy of the Polish Republic in Russia "dishonest".
The report added that the expelled diplomats must leave the territory of the Russian Federation before May 15.
