The African Union suspended Mali's membership after the coup. However, by following the principles of consensus (consensus or until all members agree) and non-interference in the internal affairs of other member countries, ASEAN may not take such action. Most ASEAN members agree that sanctions are not a solution to Myanmar's problems, but rather hurt civilians.
* Scenarios facing ASEAN
There are many political scenarios that ASEAN may face. The first scenario is to recognize the results of the November 2020 general election, which show that Aung San Suu Kyi's National League for Democracy has won a landslide victory. Recognizing the election results is a moral, political and ethical solution. But it may not happen because the Myanmar military will not accept it.
Scenario 2: Accept the idea of holding a re-election as promised by Min Aung Leng. This is a complicated choice, as it will be opposed or disbanded by Suu Kyi's party, and some experts believe that the election held by the Myanmar military will not solve the current political crisis, but only create problems. In addition, there were protests and violence.
In doing so, Min Aung Leng wants the ASEAN concession to allow the bloc to agree to an investigation into allegations of vote rigging in the November 2020 general election by setting up a commission of inquiry. With members from the Myanmar military government and officials from ASEAN before a new election.
