SEOUL: More than 30 South Korean college students shaved their heads in front of the Japanese Embassy in Seoul on Tuesday to protest the Japanese government's decision to dump wastewater from the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant into the sea. This is according to the release by Reuters on Tuesday, April 20, 2021.
Scratched protesters were wrapped in white cloth with a message condemning Japan's plans to release water from the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant into the sea and calling for it to be scrapped.
"The Japanese government should immediately cancel the discharge plan," they wrote on the bandage.
Last week, the Japanese government planned to release more than 1 million tons of water from the Fukushima nuclear power plant, which was devastated by an earthquake and tsunami 10 years ago, into the Pacific Ocean over the next two years.
The decision was met with concern from the fishing community in Japan itself, as well as from neighboring countries in the region, such as South Korea and China.
