BEIJING, April 13 (Xinhua) - The Japanese government officially decided to dump the nuclear waste of the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant into the sea on April 13, despite allegations of skepticism and national and international opposition.
This is the first time in the history of the world that a country will dump waste caused by a nuclear accident directly into the sea. Japan's decision has been met with strong opposition from neighboring countries, including China and South Korea.
The Chinese Foreign Ministry issued a statement urging Japan not to dump waste into the sea arbitrarily before full dialogue and reaching consensus with relevant countries. "Close interests and the International Atomic Energy Agency."
It is noteworthy that the US-Japan Leaders' Summit included the Taiwan issue in the Joint Statement for the first time, a major trend. Sensitive issues in Sino-Japanese relations There is no doubt that this will have a negative impact on Sino-Japanese relations in the future.
In recent years, Japan has been working hard and moving in the same direction with China, and Sino-Japanese relations are on the right track, gradually moving towards normalization. Why is Japan now suddenly changing lanes?
Some Japanese politicians need to be clear that the Taiwan issue is relevant to China's core interests. If Japan wants to violate China's red line on this issue with the United States, then what is expected will be a strong response. Advise Japan: Stay away from the Taiwan issue. The Japanese politicians in front of them and behind them will eventually accept their interests.
