Ministry of Public Works Issues 3 Guidelines to Subordinates to Participate in the Implementation of Traffic Control Measures from One Area to Another
(Phnom Penh): To participate in the implementation of the Royal Government's measures to prevent and control the spread of Kovid-19 virus, especially during the implementation of the curfew from one area to another as stated in Resolution No. 49 Pillar dated On April 14, 2021, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport has issued three guidelines for officials under the Ministry to effectively implement.
* First, all members of the Infrastructure Project Unit (PMU-PIU) of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, as well as construction technical consultants and construction companies, must carry out their work in each construction site. Be on duty and do not move or roam to other target areas during the implementation of the above curfew measures.
* Second, to temporarily suspend the mission of civil servants from the national level to the provincial level, requiring all civil servants on construction projects stationed in other provinces to continue to perform their duties in Each site will consistently implement the Royal Government's curfew measures and the curfew measures of the provincial authorities in which they are located.
* Third: In case of necessity to complete the mission without interruption during the implementation of the curfew measures, the issuance of the mission order to visit the necessary work will be determined by the Senior Minister, Minister of Public Works and Direct delivery or the right to sign by Mr. Suy San, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport only.
The following are the guidelines of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport: General Knowledge: Brunei in the Colonial Period (Part 9)
(Brunei): In the eighth episode, you probably already know about the colonial events in Indonesia, and in the ninth episode, we would like to mention the colonial events in Brunei, because Brunei used to be a kingdom. Also glorious in Southeast Asia, the islands, but due to the royal conflict in the country and the colonial rule, the Islamic empire gradually weakened.
Brunei flourished and became better known during the 15th and 16th centuries. Brunei is famous for its resources, especially spices, carbon and finished products, which are well known in the West. On the other hand, Brunei has exported huge sums of gold to the country, making it one of Southeast Asia's major trading hubs.
In addition to the prosperity of economic power, Brunei is also a center for the propagation of Islam, forcing the northern coastal towns of Borneo to embrace Islam and co-operate in the traditional way of life. Brunei specialties such as receptions and wedding traditions.
Britain pledged to Brunei that while Brunei was under British rule, Brunei would still have a monarch, with a normal dynasty and the freedom to rule the country. The British government will help oversee foreign affairs on behalf of Brunei. The British government has the right to establish a consulate and appoint civil servants to Brunei at any time.
Britain has the special right to receive business and shipping benefits from Brunei, not to hand over territory to other countries. The British and those under British rule would be privileged in Brunei. From being a colony until now, Brunei has never had a problem seizing power.
