Opposition groups call for new government in Myanmar
RANGON (Reuters) - Opposition groups in Myanmar's military junta on Friday announced the formation of a new government with the main goal of seeking international support and recognition. This was released by Reuters on Friday evening, April 16, 2021.
The National Unity Government of Myanmar has unveiled a number of components, including former MP Aung San Suu Kyi, as well as protest leaders and members of ethnic minorities. The government's Minister for International Cooperation told reporters that the goal is to end violence, restore democracy and build a democratic federal union.
Myanmar has been in a state of turmoil since the February 1, 2021 coup that toppled Suu Kyi's civilian government after a landslide victory in the second term elections. November last year, as the military accused the election of being rigged.
As of Friday, there were no reports of the situation in Myanmar, such as the usual demonstrations and crackdowns. The Myanmar military's response to the formation of a new government by opposition groups has not been seen.
