Mr. Kim Santepheap also explained that the closure is a strict administrative measure that requires all residents to stay at home, suspend travel, suspend all gatherings, and companies or enterprises to temporarily suspend operations. The closing period is 14 days. Travel is only possible for certain necessities and emergencies, such as going out to buy food, sports or going to the doctor.
Meetings may be held only when absolutely necessary, such as funerals or activities for public welfare and social benefit. Gatherings of families living together in their own homes are also permitted.
All companies or enterprises that are necessary for the daily life of the general population can still operate, such as shops, food markets, factories or handicrafts, food manufacturers, hospitals or private clinics, banks and so on.
Enterprises or companies that are not the unavoidable needs of the general public must suspend all activities. But public institutions that provide public services to the people continue to operate.
All public and private institutions that are allowed to continue to operate must reduce the number of employees to a minimum and must implement all health measures such as wearing masks, keeping safety gaps and washing hands.
