The White House and the Kremlin have reported the second round of talks between the two presidents since Biden took office in January after Western officials urged Moscow to end military action. Moscow has said its enemies should keep US warships away from the Crimea.
Concerned about the uncontrollable tensions in the Ukraine crisis, Biden called Putin to ask them to meet in a third country, and Biden reiterated the United States' commitment. For the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Ukraine.
"President Biden has also made it clear that the United States will work hard to protect its national interests in response to Russia's actions, such as interference," the White House said in a statement. "Into the internet and interfering in the election."
"US President Biden has expressed concern over the immediate increase in Russian military forces in the Crimea and along the border with Ukraine, and called on Russia to reduce tensions," the statement said.
The United States and the European Union accused Russia of annexing Crimea from Ukraine in 2014. Fighting has escalated in recent weeks in eastern Ukraine, where government forces have been battling Russian-backed separatists in a seven-year conflict that Kyiv says. Killed at least 14,000 people.
