In the first quarter of this year, the General Department of Customs collected 614 million USD, down 15%
The meeting to review the work of Customs and Excise in March and the first quarter of 2021 was chaired by Dr. Kun Nhim, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister, Director General of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia, with the participation of the Deputy Director General Departments, branch managers, office heads and some customs and excise officials throughout the country. The meeting was held on April 12, 2021 and was held on video.
The General Department of Customs confirmed that the revenue collection results in the first quarter of 2021 are 26% of the annual plan set by the Law on Finance for 2021 management.
If we look at the provisional figures of international trade volume in the first quarter of 2021 compared to the first quarter of 2020 are as follows:
* Exports of non-garment investment products (such as bicycles, electronic components, wiring, automobile parts, lamps, lamps, plastics and other industrial products) amounted to USD 887.68 million, an increase of approximately USD 300.93 million. Or 52.18%. The positive growth in exports of non-garment investment products reflects the results of the implementation of the Royal Government's policy on industrial diversification in Cambodia.
The Minister further instructed all officials under the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia to pay close attention to strengthening active, proactive and intelligent revenue collection, and to use all intellectual potential to implement revenue plans. Completed and exceeded the plan, while at the same time maintaining the sustainability of work and ensuring the safety of officers and subordinates by adhering to 3 health measures and 3 protections.
The Minister also instructed the leaders of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia to continue to participate in social work during this difficult time by continuing to implement humanitarian / charity campaigns to alleviate the hardships of vulnerable people such as orphans. Poor people and their families who are obligated to do so.
