Philippines, US to launch two-week joint military exercise starting April 12
The Philippine and US military will hold two weeks of joint military exercises starting Monday, April 12, after canceling the joint annual training event last year due to the COVID outbreak. 19. This is according to the release by AFP on Sunday, April 11, 2021.
The announcement came after the two countries' defense secretaries held a telephone conversation to discuss the exercise and the situation in the South China Sea, as well as recent regional security developments.
Unlike previous exercises, this year's Balikatan military exercise, which is designed to test the army's readiness to respond to threats such as natural disasters and extremist attacks, will Is reduced.
Relations between Washington and its former colony have been strained since 2016, when Philippine President Duterte criticized US foreign policy as friendship with China improved.
Last year, Duterte decided to unilaterally cancel the two countries' military agreement, known as the VFA, in an angry response after one of its allies was denied a visa by the United States. The agreement provides a legal framework for US troops to operate on a rotating basis in the Philippines. However, the two countries still have two defense agreements.
