General Knowledge: Colonial United States (Part 4)
(USA): From the beginning of the 17th century, the United States was under the colonization of many European powers, but with the unification of the 13 colonies, they also demanded independence from Britain and officially declared independence. In 1776 AD. Since then, the new federal state has gained more and more territory and has become a modern superpower to this day.
It should be informed that in this new article, we would like to continue the general knowledge article "500 years of the colonial world", part 1, part 2 and part 3, which have already been published. Want to highlight the events that took place during the European colonization of the United States until independence from Britain.
On December 16, 1773, British settlers in Boston, Massachusetts, disguised themselves as Indians, dropped a package of tea from a British ship into the sea out of anger. The London government, which put more pressure on them. London authorities responded by closing the Port of Boston to British British settlers in the United States, and continued to levy taxes.
In May 1775, the settlers revolted against the London government, attacking a group of British officials stationed in the United States. This event marked the beginning of the United States' independence war. On July 4, 1776, the leaders of the 13 colonies convened in Philadelphia, declaring independence from Britain.
Through the Treaty of Versailles, 1783 (Versailles, France), the British government recognized the independence of the 13 colonies and ceded Apalas, Mississippi, including the western Mississippi, to the settlers. Even more so.
