Commentary: What is the purpose of the United States to inflate the South China Sea issue again?
But under US incitement, the usual actions of Chinese fishermen were deliberately inflated into so-called "Chinese naval militant fishing vessels". Peace of mind for a while now.
In fact, the number of Chinese fishing boats avoiding the wind there has dwindled. Even the Philippine media acknowledged that from the more than 200 fishing vessels mentioned by the Philippine side in early March, to more than 180 vessels, down to dozens today.
This year marks the 30th anniversary of the establishment of China-ASEAN dialogue relations in early April at the invitation of Wang Yi, Member of the State Council and Foreign Minister of China, Foreign Minister of the four ASEAN Member States, Singapore and Malaysia. Indonesia and the Philippines have repeatedly visited China.
China and the four countries discussed in-depth issues such as promoting the implementation of the RCEP agreement and cooperation in the prevention and control of Kovid 19. And the RCEP is an issue that the United States has always been concerned about.
In general, the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP), initiated by 10 ASEAN countries, is a free trade area established by 15 countries, including China, Japan, South Korea, Australia and New Zealand. Original.
The agreement covers about 47 percent of the global population, about 32 percent of the world's gross domestic product, about 29 percent of global trade and about 32 percent of global investment. The good news is that all the member states of the RCEP have indicated that they will push for the agreement to enter into force on January 1, 2022.
So I think you can clearly understand why the United States is so concerned about this agreement. Why is the US propaganda about the South China Sea now? The signing of the RCEP means that ASEAN and its dialogue partners support free trade and say "no" to protectionism.
To be honest, it is true that there are US allies in the RCEP, but there is no relationship with the United States and no relationship with the US dollar. If the situation in which the countries in the region live in peace, mutual benefit and win-win can continue, the United States will see that this agreement will hinder its global economic hegemony.
BERLIN: The European Court of Human Rights (ECHR) ruling is now the first time the tribunal has weighed in on the issue of mandatory vaccination. This decision could certainly play a role in efforts to end the spread of viral infections. This is according to the release on the German website DW on April 8, 2021.
The decision comes after complaints were lodged with some Czech families over restrictions on immunization of children. Although this decision is not directly related to the COVID-19 vaccine, some experts believe that "this measure may affect the vaccination process against the virus, especially for those who refuse to be vaccinated." COVID-19 ”.
Despite the ECHR ruling, European countries may not force their people to be vaccinated.
