Speaking in a video message today, Samdech Techo Hun Sen expressed his intention to want the United States to follow the example of the Japanese government. Samdech Techo Hun Sen praised Japan's attitude in finding all possible ways to help Cambodia. Samdech said that while Japan could not provide loans to Cambodia, Japan provided various goods to sell in Cambodia and left the money for the development of the country.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen said that he had proposed this point three times to Mr. Obama, but was told by Obama that it was difficult to explain to the US Congress.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen used to consider the US debt that Cambodia owed during the Lon Nol coup was a dirty debt that forced Cambodia to buy US bombs and drop them on the heads of Cambodians, causing many deaths and injuries. Not all. Remembering this story every time, Samdech stated that it is the most painful thing for all Cambodians.
The United States supported and lent money to the Lon Nol regime, which staged an illegal coup to oust Norodom Sihanouk in 1970. Some documents show that the loan was only about $ 270 million, but if you add up the interest, so far the loan has risen to more than $ 600 million.
In the past, the current Royal Government of Cambodia has asked the United States to consider turning these debts into grants to help develop Cambodia as a result of Lon Nol's coup overthrowing Norodom Sihanouk in Cambodia. It caused a devastating war, and US planes dropped millions of bombs, killing and injuring many Cambodians. However, the United States has come forward to reject the proposal.
According to a report by the Cambodian Mine Action Center (CMAC), the United States dropped 2.8 million tons of bombs and chemical bombs on Cambodia from early 1963 to early 1975 under the pretext of destroying the Yeak Kong. Vietnam invaded but killed, but more than 500,000 Cambodians were killed by the bombs, including tens of thousands of Cambodian villages and homes damaged.
Chemical bombs, a legacy of the United States, have also continued to threaten the lives of Cambodians past and present. Many chemical bombs were found in Svay Rieng and other provinces such as Mondulkiri and Tbong Khmum. Hundreds of people were found to have been affected by the bombing, which required immediate action.
