Japan successfully completes first lung transplant from survivor to Kovid-19 with severe lung damage
The woman, who received a lung tissue donation, was in stable condition after a nearly 11-hour operation on Wednesday, according to a statement from Kyoto University Hospital. Her husband and children, who donated parts of their lungs, are also in stable condition.
Kyoto University claims it is the world's first successful lung transplant with COVID-19 from a surviving donor. For similar surgeries, only lung tissue from a donor who is considered dead has been performed in the United States, China and Europe.
Her husband and son also volunteered to donate parts of their lungs, with the husband giving part of the left lung and the son giving part of the right lung. The operation was performed at Kyoto University Hospital by a team of 30 doctors. The woman is expected to be released from the hospital in about two months and return to normal in about three months.
