APSARA Authority plans to restore odd pond in front of Angkor Wat
(Siem Reap): The odd pond in front of Angkor Wat temple has started to gradually change shape after the APSARA Authority facilitated the relocation of stalls from the area around the pond by the end of 2020. Currently, a project to develop and improve the odd pond is underway, and archaeologists are conducting excavations to study further possibilities for restoring and rehabilitating the ancient pond.
Thoeun Sokhon, an archeology officer with the Department of Temple Preservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology, said his excavation team had found the shape of the Trapeang Ses Dam as multi-layered in the structure of the dam. Mr. Sokhon's team excavated 11 excavation sites and found some pottery, as well as a clearer view of the dam structure around the pond.
He described the dam being lifted up, the clay removed, the sand mixed with dry gravel, then the clay used as a core to make the dam last longer, and then a layer of sand and compacted. Using crushed limestone mixed with clay.
According to Mr. Thoeun Sokhon, APSARA Authority plans to restore the odd pond soon to store more water than before for the benefit of both the temple and the people around the area.
Chou Radina, deputy director of the Department of Forest Management, Landscape, Culture and Environment, says his team is busy planting grass and trees around the odd pond, which is planned to cover a total area of more than 30,000 square meters. Currently, about 40 percent of the work is done.
The project, which started in early February, has been a bit slow due to the work around Trapeang Ses, a former stall and the use of cars driving, causing the ground to be rocky and difficult for gardeners to work on. Land. Mr. Chouradina added that the odd pond area is an archeological site that needs to be excavated before preparing anything, so his team will speed up the garden after the archaeologists study.
