TOKYO (Reuters) - Sino-Russian relations have soured in recent years, reaching a new stage, leading to signs that some experts believe the two neighbors are at loggerheads. Start forming your own coalition or bloc to counter the US-led Western countries.
The meeting between Wang Yi and Sergei Lavrov came on the same day that European and US countries announced new sanctions on China over Hong Kong and Xinjiang, while Russia was also under European and US sanctions. Instead, these sanctions make China and Russia even sweeter.
* Create your own party?
In fact, when it comes to Sino-Russian relations in Asia, especially in Central Asia, the two giants are more at odds than at odds. Russia, on the other hand, fears that China's growing influence could undermine its influence in the region, while China is also unhappy with Russia, as Moscow is a major arms supplier to India, a rival. China's right in border dispute case
China-Russia trade volume increased from 10 percent in 2013 to 18 percent in 2020, despite falling oil prices due to the COVID-19 outbreak. China is currently the largest market for Russian goods, which could partially ease Russia's burden from US and European sanctions.
Both China and Russia need each other. With the help of Russia, China would be stronger, and without Russia, China would simply be a powerless ally. Russia needs China because it can ease the economic burden or reduce the economic impact of Western sanctions.
