Research Article: Concerned Child Sexual Abuse Online
The large number of internet users is a good thing, but it is also a worrying thing. Worrying because more and more children and young people are also using the internet and they are the most vulnerable. In 2019, UNICEF found that 85.7 percent of young Cambodians using the internet had experienced violence, harassment, and cyberbullying.
The organization also states that in Cambodia, 80.4 percent of girls and 91.5 percent of boys aged 15-24 use the Internet regularly Meanwhile, Save the Children International interviewed 730 parents about their knowledge of the risks that children face when using the Internet. Seventy percent of them are unaware that children using the internet may be at risk, especially when it comes to online sexual abuse.
An increase in the number of children and young people using the Internet, combined with a lack of parental knowledge, will make children and youth more vulnerable.
Mitchell's research shows that, on average, criminals spend more than a month trying to lure a child or young victim into sexual activity. Mr. Brid acknowledged that it may take more than a month to persuade a child victim to fall in love, but if only to be able to make a face-to-face meeting with the child victim, the gang takes less than a week. .]. Generally, their contact with child victims does not exceed 180 days.
At this stage, even if the child victim agrees to have sex with him, the perpetrator will continue to make plans, such as extorting money, extortion, or even forcing him to have sex with another partner (sex trafficking) in exchange for Do not take pictures or videos to advertise.
