ECCC Considers Appeal Against Co-Investigating Judges' Closing Order in Case 003
(Phnom Penh): The Pre-Trial Chamber of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) has considered the appeal against the Co-Investigating Judges' Closing Order in Case 003.
On 28 November 2018, the International Co-Investigating Judge issued a Closing Order (Trial Order), sending Meas Muth to trial separately. On the same day, the National Co-Investigating Judge issued an acquittal order. Against Meas Muth (indictment). This is according to the press release received by Fresh News on April 7, 2021.
Consensus section unanimously decided by the Pre-Trial Chamber After reviewing the case file, the Pre-Trial Chamber ruled that the appeals of the National Co-Prosecutors and the International Co-Prosecutors were acceptable (paragraph 51 and 53 of Consideration) The appeal of Meas Muth's co-defense was declared unacceptable pursuant to Rule 74 (3) (a) and 21 of the Internal Rules (paragraphs 74 and 77).
Regarding the validity of the Closing Order, the International Judge clarified that in case of inconsistencies between the Co-Investigating Judges on whether to issue a referral order or a waiver order containing Within the legal framework of the ECCC, the principle of continuation of indictment requires that a subpoena be issued as requested (paragraphs 257 and 261).
Therefore, the dismissal order, in addition to the annulment of the facts, also constitutes an attempt to eliminate this actual position, which is therefore beyond the scope of the authority (paragraphs 262 and 284), while the order to send the case to trial Clearing remains primarily valid and in accordance with the legal framework in force (paragraphs 262 and 284).
After claiming that Meas Muth was among those most responsible for crimes committed by the Khmer Rouge (paragraph 340), the International Judge concluded that it complied with Rule 77 (13) of the Rules of Procedure of the Trial Chamber. The court shall accept the summons to the International Co-Investigating Judge (paragraphs 342-334).
The Banking and Financial Institutions stated that "Kovid 19 infected customers will be exempted from paying interest and all fines for one month and will automatically receive a credit refinement (if the client agrees). ) Including a three-month deferral of principal payments. "Every customer infected with Kovid 19 will have to show a positive Kovid 19 test result issued by the competent authorities."
At the same time, for customers who are engaged in debt consolidation, the loan will be provided automatically (if the customer agrees), as well as deferring the payment of both principal and interest for one month and waiving all penalties. Continue to provide credit restructuring quickly and conveniently for clients affected by Kovid 19.
At the same time, the banking and financial institutions also announced the issuance of emergency or additional loans by easing the conditions and reducing interest rates to help continue and rehabilitate the family economy and business before and after the crisis, as well as prepare loans. In addition, all penalties are waived for customers who purchase a First Home Owner.
In particular, for clients who have died of Kovid-19, banking and financial institutions will provide benefits according to the actual capabilities of each institution.
It should be noted that as of the end of February 2021, about 340,000 customers have received credit refinancing in the amount of about 4 billion US dollars. As for the credit restructuring mechanism, most of them choose to pay interest only for 3 to 6 months, extend the loan repayment period or suspend both interest and principal for 3 to 6 months. Cases where customers are severely affected.
