(Phnom Penh): The Banking and Financial Institutions through the Banking Association of Cambodia (ABC) and the Cambodian Microfinance Association on April 7, 2021 issued a joint statement by launching additional interventions to support customers during Outbreak of Kovid-19 disease.
The association's joint statement said that over the past year, Cambodian banking and financial institutions have been actively involved in helping clients affected by Kovid-19 disease through credit restructuring. As well as mitigation and other concessions from the Royal Government of Cambodia, regulators and relevant authorities.
In the wake of previous efforts, it has been observed that since the February 20 community event, the outbreak of Kovid-19 has caused further repercussions, with the number of cases and the risk of infection increasing.
Given this impact, banking and financial institutions through the Banking Association of Cambodia (ABC) and the Cambodian Microfinance Association (CMA), all member institutions must continue to fully implement the Royal Government's measures, especially the principles In a credit restructuring launched in February 2020 by the National Bank of Cambodia to ensure that its customers receive protection and favors during this difficult time.
In order to support and facilitate customers in this difficult time, both associations set out the following additional principles:
1. For Kovid-19 infected customers: Exemption from interest payments and all fines for one month, as well as automatic credit refinancing (if the client agrees), including deferment of the principal payment period. Three months. All customers infected with Kovid-19 must present a positive Kovid-19 test result issued by the competent authority.
2. For Custodian customers: Provides automatic loan readjustment (if the client agrees), including a one-month deferral of principal and interest payments and a waiver of all penalties.
3. Continue to provide credit restructuring quickly and conveniently for clients affected by Kovid-19
4. Issue emergency or supplementary loans by easing conditions and lowering interest rates to help sustain and rehabilitate the family economy and business before and after the crisis.
6. Provide preference for clients who have died of Kovid-19 according to the actual possibilities of each institution.
It should also be noted that all banking and financial institutions have a similar management structure, the source of capital is about 15% of the shareholders' own capital, about 15% of the capital from long-term domestic and foreign loans (the bank is also a subsidiary). Debts as well) and capital from customers and the public who have deposited about 70%.
