In 2020, a Malaysian court ordered Najib, who is currently out of jail and a member of parliament, to pay $ 409 million in taxes owed between 2011 and 2017. As of Tuesday this week, Najib himself had said that tax officials had issued a letter requesting that they come in and otherwise they would file bankruptcy proceedings against him.
If Najib is declared bankrupt, the 67-year-old former Malaysian prime minister could lose his seat in parliament and be barred from running in the election. However, Najib claimed that he had always paid taxes, considering that the charges against him were politically motivated.
Najib Razak lost power in 2018 after his party lost the general election at the time. After losing power, he was indicted and sentenced to 12 years in prison for abuse of power and corruption of billions of dollars in state funds. Najib had already filed an appeal against the verdict earlier this week, stating that he had not committed any wrongdoing.
