Commentary: Adhere to the spirit of diligence and perseverance, the poverty alleviation work is successful
This reiterates the view of some analysts that China's absolute poverty reduction is "the largest human rights project in the world" and "the best practice in the field of human rights." Also. In particular, this year marks the 100th anniversary of the founding of the Communist Party of China. Since its inception, the Chinese Communist Party has considered the task of finding happiness for the Chinese people and restoring the prosperity of the Chinese nation as its task.
From building a new, people-centered China to implementing reform and opening-up policies, to phasing out poverty reduction plans in stages, generations of Chinese Communist Party members have persevered. Try and persevere until the goal is achieved successfully.
China's efforts to eradicate poverty and ensure human rights have been praised around the world. Martin Jacques, a senior researcher at the University of Cambridge in the United Kingdom, described the eradication of poverty as a "great achievement" of the Chinese Communist Party in transforming China.
In the context of epidemics and unprecedented changes over the past hundred years, China's successful implementation and valuable experience in poverty reduction have contributed to China's plan to reduce poverty and Global governance has also complemented China's momentum in promoting the protection of human rights around the world. General Knowledge: 500 Years of Colonial World (Part 1)
(World): For more than 500 years, European powers have pursued colonial policies that dominated countries around the world. These powers are divided into two major groups: the old powers, which are Spain, Portugal, and the Netherlands, while the new powers are Germany, Britain, and France.
1: The old superpower
From the 15th century, the Spanish and Portuguese empires first invaded some territories in the Americas, then conquered parts of Africa and then conquered the states of Asia.
In 1565, the Spanish Empire conquered the Cebu Kingdom and later established the capital Manila in 1571 under Spanish rule.
The Netherlands, on the other hand, made the long voyage by sea to Japan in 1600 AD. About 19 years later, the Dutch seized the capital, Jakarta, or Jakarta, from the Javanese Muslim kingdom and renamed the city Jakarta "Pattavia".
The presence of these three colonial armies did not seem to be very competitive and did not invade the mainland kingdoms yet. Most of their locations are on the coast and on small islands. It was not until the arrival of Britain and France that they arrived in Asia in the 18th century, and colonial events on the continent began to escalate.
