The Minister of Health told the Capital-Provincial Governor to prepare a team of doctors to care for and treat 19 Kovid patients at home (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Prof. Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health of Cambodia on April 6, 2021 Sent a letter to all provincial governors to prepare a team of doctors to care for and treat 19 Kovid patients at home.
The letter from the Minister of Health follows a recommendation from the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, to the Ministry of Health and other stakeholders to consider in advance the possibility of providing home treatment for 19 patients with mild to moderate conditions.
The letter from the Minister of Health stated that in order to alleviate the burden of public hospitals in the capital-province, the Ministry proposed to set up a team of doctors to provide home care and treatment for 19 asymptomatic Kovid patients and cases of mild illness in the condition. There is a serious epidemic eventually beyond the capacity of public hospitals or clinics that each capital-province has already set up.
Mr. Mam Bunheng added that the reserve should be carried out by a team of doctors at his own health facility, organized as a team and led by the director of the OD health office.
The Ministry of Health will train the doctors in accordance with the Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) that the Ministry will provide. This is according to Mr. Mam Bunhet.
It should be noted that Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, has issued a new guideline for the Ministry of Health and other stakeholders to consider and prepare in advance the possibility of providing home treatment for 19 Kovid patients with mild conditions.
In a voice message shared on social media on the morning of April 6, Samdech Techo considered that this is a consideration and a plan in case the number of patients increases and can not be located for treatment. .
Samdech Techo Hun Sen's initiative to consider home treatment comes after the 19 communicable disease outbreak in the February 20 community event continued to escalate.
