General Knowledge: Discover the history of the Swedish canal where a giant ship was stranded.
* History of the Suez Canal
Before 1859, the Mediterranean Sea and the Red Sea were separated by a short continental barrier over the borders of Egypt. All the goods that want to be transported to these two seas need to be transported by land, but this land transport is often robbed by thieves who often rob goods and property from both capitalists. That.
In 1859, the excavation of the new canal began gradually, but tragically, tens of thousands of workers were forced to work hard using only hoe and manual labor. Later, the French government used machinery instead to help speed up this important canal digging process.
In 1875, the United Kingdom bought a stake in the canal, as it was the mainstay of British navigation to Asia. Britain also promised to help Egypt gain independence from the Ottoman Empire (present-day Turkey), but in return Egypt had to hand over control of the Suez Canal to British rule.
At present, there are an average of 50 large cargo ships passing through the Suez Canal every day, with a total weight of more than 300,000,000 tons per year. Cabinet meeting scheduled for April 9 postponed
