A piece of Vishnu's head was found in Trapeang Phong temple, Siem Reap province
The head piece of Vishnu above has one right side of the face, on which there are the rest of the wings, eyebrows, eyes, nose, lips, and one ear. At the top is a cylindrical crown that covers the forehead along the hairline around the front to the back. This piece of statue was found by the team repairing Trapeang Phong temple on March 31, 2021.
Chhouk Somala, an art registration officer with the Department of Preservation of Temples in Angkor Park and the Preventive Archeology of the Apsara Authority, said that the head fragments of the statue were still part of the head, which could be identified as a head. Vishnu or Narayan, that is, not Shiva or Iso, nor Harihara (the god who synthesizes between Vishnu and Shiva).
According to him, this statue seems to be in the Kulen style, with the faces of the statues large, short, the eyebrows not separated, and even more distinctive is the high cylindrical crown on top, which is characteristic of the statue. In the Kulen style.
After the record was handed over to the Heritage Police and the travel agency, the sculpture was temporarily stored at the APSARA Authority Center for registration and further search. Other pieces may be repaired in the coming days.
