Myanmar military airstrikes force more than 12,000 civilians to flee their homes
Rangoon: Indigenous Karen armed groups on April 3 accused the Myanmar military of using excessive force, continuing to launch airstrikes, forcing more than 12,000 civilians in the country. That includes children becoming refugees, leaving their villages and homes. According to a release by CNA.
In March, armed groups of the Karen National Union (KNU) seized a military base in Karen State and killed 10 Myanmar military officials. In retaliation, the Myanmar military launched airstrikes on several Karen armed bases.
The KNU on Saturday strongly condemned the Myanmar military for its excessive use of force through the continued bombing and airstrikes from March 27 to March 30, which left civilians dead. Many, including children, lost their lives. The attack also sparked a serious humanitarian crisis as more than 12,000 civilians were forced to flee their villages and homes.
Demonstrators in Myanmar continue to protest against the military regime, which is stepping up its crackdown on street protesters and its critics online. More than 550 people have been killed since the coup on February 1, and 3,000 civilians fled to Thailand on Monday, most of them. Returned to Myanmar, which Thai authorities claim was voluntary.
