General knowledge: More than ten thousand concubines are buried alive in the tomb of Emperor Qingxi to accompany the emperor
Today, our media unit would like to share some general knowledge related to the tomb and Emperor Qing Xihang to the following readers:
The tomb of the king was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1987. According to historians, there are many claims that Emperor Qing Xihang was born in 259 BC and died in 210 BC.
According to the Qing Dynasty tradition, the emperor had to prepare the stupa in advance, so the stupa was built at the foot of Mount Lisan, now in the Lintong area of Xi'an, Shaanxi Province, and construction began at the same time. Emperor Qing Xihang had just ascended the throne at the age of 13.
According to the history books, this stupa is rectangular in shape, four corners long, 5 liters or 415 meters long and 166 meters high, which inside looks like an underground palace. There are hundreds of palaces in the palace, including statues of Amat and many officials sitting in the courtroom.
Above the stupa is a replica of a natural forest with peaks made of pearls, statues of peacocks and cranes made of gold and silver. The roof of the stupa is designed as a replica of the sky, with the sun, moon and stars made of glittering jewels, as well as mechanical keys for attacking unauthorized intruders.
The Great Stupa is filled with statues of soldiers and war horses made of clay, the size of a real statue, arranged in a procession around the cemetery in the same tactical manner as the war. In addition, there are diamonds, sapphires, gems, precious stones, skeletons of servants and the skeletons of animals that are sacrificed. This huge stupa took at least 39 years to build and sacrificed up to 720,000 workers.
Although built in the late 3rd century BC, these statues were only discovered in 1974. In 1974, farmers in Ling Tong dug a well and found the statues.
* About the death of the emperor
Desiring to enjoy the immortality of the emperor, King Qingxi ordered the scholars to seek immortality medicine, but as we all know, no human being can have immortality, as the scholars say. One phrase says, "Only change does not change." Towards the end of the Qing Dynasty, he went to explore the forest in an area called Yunmang to pray for the spirits of the ancestors of various dynasties.
