The business of selling dried oysters by cart helps Mr. An sleep to have a better life.
Keng said that although sales have fallen slightly, the business is still good enough to support his family in these difficult circumstances.
The man who sells oysters is from Kampong Cham province, but because there is no work for his family in the district, he decided to do business in Siem Reap. Originally, when he first arrived in Siem Reap, he had only 200,000 riel and bought fried banana carts from others to repair and started selling oysters from then on.
At the beginning of the sale, An Keng claimed that he did not know how to mix ingredients, sold a little per day, and later asked some neighbors how to do it, but they did not tell. After trying for a while and trying to find a way to make delicious oysters, Mr. An Keng knows how to mix ingredients little by little until he has the support of customers.
The fact that Mr. An Keng's Liel Hal has a lot of support and sells well makes his family's life prosperous, but this business is also very tiring. Every day, he has to get up at 1 a.m. to pick up clams and stains until it's time to go to the market. Mr. An's bed is from Kampong Cham, Kampong Chhnang, Kampong Thom and some from the Tonle Sap. Every day he sells three places, one in front of Nher Market, two in front of Huy Leng Market and the other east of Angkor High School.
One customer, who asked not to be named, said that she often came to buy oysters here to eat with her family because the oysters here are delicious, hygienic and have a refrigerator to keep them cool. Huy also makes her always come to buy shellfish here.
