During the Cold War, Washington helped restore Europe and formed a military alliance, NATO, to counter the former Soviet Union. In the face of China-US confrontation, most experts see that in the short or medium term, it may not be possible to mobilize allies or regional partners to form an Asian military alliance or "NATO Asia". Yes.
The biggest reason seen as an obstacle to the United States' formation of the Asian Military Alliance is "economic interests between China and Asian countries." China is not like the former Soviet Union. Unlike in China, it is now the world's second largest economy and an indispensable economic partner for countries in the region, regardless of whether it is an ally of the United States. An obvious example is Japan.
Everyone knows that Japan is an ally of the United States. But Japan has no specific economic or geopolitical reason to oppose China, except for territorial disputes in the East China Sea. Instead, Japan needs the Chinese market to recover its economy from the COVID-19 crisis, as China buys 20 percent of its products each year.
Moreover, in the eyes of the people of Asia, China does not reach the level of fear or threat to their country and the region as a whole, as the United States claims. For decades, China has never intervened militarily to invade any country, while the United States has been embroiled in bloody wars abroad, such as in Afghanistan, Syria, Libya and Iraq under the pretext of democracy and human rights.
