Ministry of Education bans public-private educational institutions from cooperating or receiving support from tobacco manufacturers
(Phnom Penh): Due to the observation that a small number of educational institutions use tobacco trademarks in their programs, the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports has issued a directive banning cooperation and cooperation between tobacco manufacturers and Units under the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, Capital-Province, Office of Education, Youth and Sports of Municipal-District-Khan Administration, educational institutions at all levels, both public and private.
This guideline is also part of the implementation of the Convention on the Control of Tobacco Products. Article 5.3 of the Convention states that public health and the tobacco industry have irreconcilable conflicts of interest because tobacco companies produce and promote tobacco products that have been scientifically found to be addictive. Illness, disability, death and other social problems, including poverty.
In addition, through the study of internal documents of international tobacco companies, the tobacco industry uses social responsibility strategies to build relationships with individuals who have the right to make decisions, influence policy making and build the company's reputation. Tobacco companies also use social assistance programs to increase confidence, increase profits and prevent the implementation of effective health protection policies.
According to a United Nations study, tobacco use is a major cause of death for some 15,000 Cambodians each year, and an estimated $ 649 million is lost.
