(Siem Reap): Pea Reang Lake is a natural lake next to the Tonle Sap Lake in Sangkat Chreav, Siem Reap City, and this lake is surrounded by flooded forest. Climate change puts the lake at risk of rapid drought during the dry season, which poses a risk for fish and birds.
Kong Mongkul, President of the Peanut Lake Ecotourism Community, said that the lake is rich in biodiversity, such as many species of birds that live and attract many national and international visitors. The lake also has a variety of conserved fish species, such as squid, trout and trout, weighing up to 8 kg.
Due to the rainy season this year, there is not much rain and the hot weather makes the lake water down quickly. The redevelopment of the Siem Reap River is also part of the reason why water in the upper reaches can not be lowered to the lower surface of Peanut Lake. This puts birds at risk of malnutrition, and fish stocks may die if not addressed in time.
