SINGAPORE: As we all know, the United States and the CIA have always tried to cover up their actions in order to avoid embarrassment in front of the international community, but some secrets have been leaked. For example, the case of leaking secrets in Singapore.
So, in order to know exactly how the CIA lost its reputation due to Singapore, please find out through the article written in the book "Southeast Asia in 20 years of the CIA war" in detail as follows:
Former Singaporean Prime Minister Lee Kuan Yew has said directly that the CIA was trying to crack down on Singapore government intelligence by spending large sums of money to bribe Singaporean officials. Lee Kuan Yew confirmed that the event took place between 1960-1961, when he was the Prime Minister of Singapore.
The CIA contacted an official of the Singapore Intelligence Agency, promising to pay a satisfactory monthly salary and guarantee that if any problems were found or found, the CIA would do everything possible. To help the Singaporean official and his family leave Singapore to live in the United States and secure a bright future there.
In the face of this enormous amount of money, Singaporean intelligence officials seem reluctant and take three days to decide. The official eventually decided not to cooperate with the CIA and even reported to his superiors Richard Coridon about the Singapore government's attempt to buy intelligence.
After receiving the report, Richard Coridon immediately informed Prime Minister Lee Kuan Yew about the case and asked for suggestions for further action. Prime Minister Lee also suggested setting traps to arrest CIA intelligence officers at the scene of the operation, as the plan was carried out in great detail and secrecy.
In an effort to avoid international embarrassment, the State Department categorically rejected the broadcast and called Prime Minister Lee Kuan Yew's statement "inaccurate, did not facilitate the situation and was playing a political game on Indonesia".
In a fit of rage, Lee Kuan Yew released a public apology from US Secretary of State Dennis Ras.
