APSARA Authority team gets rid of termites that destroy materials at temples in Angkor Park
(Siem Reap): In addition to the weather, the age of construction, plants, termites is also a problem that poses a serious risk to the temple and some other wooden facilities for use, such as stairs, poles, bars and tents for conservation work in the park. Angkor too.
Ms. Neth Simon, Conservation Officer of the Department of Temple Preservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology, said that in the temple towers, termites piled up in mounds, while the soil was carried from the foundation or the area around the temple. This factor eventually leads to holes or holes due to depletion of soil. During the rainy season, water flows into the holes, causing the foundations of the temple to collapse and the rocks to begin to fall and cause the temple to collapse.
In addition, termites destroyed some of the materials used in the temple, such as carpets, stairs, cushions against wooden sheds.
Not only that, the termites ate the trees, most of which grew in the temples, some of the trees were rotten because the termites ate them, causing them to collapse and crush the temple towers.
