Canberra: Australian trade minister on Saturday warned China against the World Trade Organization (WTO) over Beijing's decision to raise tariffs on Australian wine for up to five months. Years. This was released by the AFP news agency on Saturday, March 27, 2021.
The Chinese Ministry of Commerce on Friday announced a tariff of 116.2-218.4 percent, which will apply to wine products imported from Australia from Sunday this week. The Chinese Ministry of Commerce said the decision to raise the tariff was due to an investigation that found irregularities in the import of wine products from Australia, which could affect the Chinese market.
According to data from the Australian Government, the volume of Australian wine exports to China reached 900 million US dollars in 2019. Last year, the two countries also argued over similar product tax increases. Australia then asked the WTO to launch an investigation into China's tariff increase on its barley products.
