(Phnom Penh): This year, One Hour Day for the Planet is organized on March 27, 2021 at 8:30 pm with a digital program "Social Networking" in the spirit of solidarity with the people All over Cambodia and the world.
Under the theme "Give Love to Nature", this year's One Hour for the Planet Day focuses on summarizing the knowledge of the benefits of nature and encouraging a general online (digital) conversation about the importance of sustaining life. Let's all learn more about the daily threats to our natural environment.
Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, recalled: "Now is the time to more clearly reflect the importance and connection between nature and biodiversity to our survival and well-being." "
The Secretary of State added: "All participatory activities, more or less in accordance with the law, responsibly and honestly, are all useful in contributing to the preservation and protection of natural resources. Of our Kingdom of Cambodia to be sustainable and help prevent the loss of biodiversity, as well as reduce the impact of climate change for the benefit of people and nature.
For this year's participation, Miss Meas Soksophea, a well-known singer in the Kingdom of Cambodia, gave a voice to nature and shared messages about the environment on social media (online) and called on all Cambodians to participate in peace activities. An environment for positive change in their daily lives together.
Ms. Sok Sophea said: "In our lifetime, each of us can not do all the big things. "But we can all be determined to contribute through small, loving activities for the benefit of nature and all of us."
"Each of us has a role to play in protecting Cambodia's nature, environment and wildlife resources for the benefit of the current generation and for generations to come," he added. "Please join us in giving a voice and committing to acting positively for nature from now on," he called.
Ms. Chhum Putheavy, Earth Youth Team Leader and a student of the Department of Environmental Sciences of the Royal University of Phnom Penh, has been observing the increasing rate of participation of Cambodian youth in environmental dialogues, participation in Cambodia's biodiversity conservation activities. As well as their participation in other environmental outreach activities.
Ms. Putheavy said: "I am committed to taking positive action to protect the environment, which is our common home, and encourage all young people to join me and the Cambodian people and the world. "Together to ensure a sustainable future and to work together to address climate change and avoid the loss of our natural resources."
For the private sector, this campaign is a great platform for them to participate as part of the essential solutions for building a healthy, well-off and sustainable society by giving back to nature and the people and communities they provide. Business services to that.
Mr. Seila added: "Every activity, small or large, is useful! I am committed to acting positively for nature. "Let's work together to provide a sustainable future for our children and future generations."
Tycoon Srey Chanthorn does not charge 500 houses for 3 months to alleviate the hardships of workers and traders
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Oknha Srey Chanthan, General Manager of Seven AG (7NG), has decided not to charge the rent of 500 houses for 3 months from April 1, 2021 to alleviate the hardships of workers and traders who are Victims of the Kovid 19 Outbreak. The house that the tycoon declared not to accept the rent is located in the company's development area in Vihear Sour, Khsach Kandal district, Kandal province.
The decision not to charge rent or room for rent came after the call of Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen. For Techo Hun Sen, these gestures are considered as exemplary humanitarian gestures to help society in times of crisis.
So far, it has been observed that many people have rented houses and rooms and have announced that they will not charge rent for houses and rooms from workers. Non-payment of rent and housing has reduced the hardships and costs of workers during the period when their earnings plummeted due to the Kovid 19 crisis.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen issued a series of letters of thanks to landlords and tenants who volunteered to share their hardships with workers.
